1) Basic derivative filters (Sobel, Prewitt)
The code applies Sobel and Prewitt filters to detect edges by measuring changes in pixel intensity. 
These filters highlight horizontal and vertical edges in the image.

2) Canny detector (multi-stage)
The code applies the Canny edge detector to identify clear edges. 
It uses noise reduction, gradient calculation and edge tracking to produce thin and connected edges.

3) Laplacian of Gaussian (LoG)
The code first smooths the image with a Gaussian filter and then applies the Laplacian operator to detect edges. 
This helps reduce noise before edge detection.

4) Edge map → segmentation (Otsu threshold)
The code uses Otsu's method to automatically calculate a threshold and convert the image into a binary image. 
This separates detected foreground regions from the background.

5) Label and visualize regions
The code identifies connected white regions in the binary image and assigns each region a label. 
It also measures properties such as area and centroid and displays the detected regions.

Reflections:
1) Which operator gives the thinnest, cleanest edges?
Canny usually produces the thinnest and cleanest edges because it uses non-maximum suppression and hysteresis thresholding.

2) Why does Canny outperform simple gradient filters?
Canny uses several processing stages to reduce noise, remove weak edges and connect meaningful edges.
Sobel and Prewitt mainly detect intensity changes and can produce thicker or noisier edges.

3) How does Otsu relate to histogram-based thresholding?
Otsu analyses the image intensity histogram and automatically selects a threshold that separates pixels into two groups by maximizing the difference between their variances.
